WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.375
Merge sort has two different jobs. First, split the index range until each part contains one value. The split itself does not rearrange the array. A one value range is already sorted.

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:31.625
The left pair, seven and three, returns as three, seven. The right pair, five and two, returns as two, five. Only now may the parent merge. These are two separate sorted inputs, not one sorted array.

00:00:31.625 --> 00:00:46.292
Compare the front values: three and two. Three is not less than or equal to two, so copy two from the right into the first temporary cell. Advance only the right pointer. Three must wait.

00:00:46.292 --> 00:01:01.625
Now compare three with five. Choose three from the left. Copy it after two and advance the left pointer. The right pointer stays on five. Sorted inputs mean only these two fronts can be next.

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:12.708
Seven is larger than five. Copy five from the right. Now the right run is exhausted. There is no second key left to compare against seven.

00:01:12.708 --> 00:01:29.167
Copy the remaining seven from the left. This is a leftover copy, not another key comparison. Temporary output is now two, three, five, seven. The merge used three key comparisons and four temporary writes.

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:49.583
Finally, copy temporary output back into the original range. Four more array writes. Only then does the parent return its sorted result. Merging copies values into a separate output. It does not swap the two fronts. On equal values, choose the left first to keep the sort stable.
